---
import Alert from '@ds/components/feedback/Alert.astro';
import Table from '@ds/components/ui/Table.astro';
// The samples are real files, compiled against @ctff/sdk and @ctff/nestjs by the `typecheck` target.
import appAsyncModule from '../../../samples/nestjs/app-async.module.ts?raw';
import appModule from '../../../samples/nestjs/app.module.ts?raw';
import contextResolver from '../../../samples/nestjs/birder-context.resolver.ts?raw';
import sightingController from '../../../samples/nestjs/sighting.controller.ts?raw';
import evaluate from '../../../samples/sdk/evaluate.ts?raw';
import CodeSample from '../../components/CodeSample.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';

const servedReasons = [
  { reason: "{ kind: 'OFF' }", when: 'Targeting is off, so the off variation is served' },
  { reason: "{ kind: 'TARGET_MATCH' }", when: 'The context’s user_id is in an individual target' },
  { reason: "{ kind: 'RULE_MATCH', ruleIndex }", when: 'Rule ruleIndex (counting from 0) is the first rule that matched' },
  { reason: "{ kind: 'DEFAULT' }", when: 'Targeting is on and no individual target or rule matched: the default variation' },
];

const fallbackReasons = [
  { reason: "{ kind: 'FLAG_NOT_FOUND' }", when: 'The environment has no flag with that key, or it is archived' },
  { reason: 'UNKNOWN_SDK_KEY', when: 'The Evaluator doesn’t know the SDK key' },
  { reason: 'NOT_READY', when: 'The Evaluator hasn’t loaded the environment’s snapshot yet' },
  { reason: 'TIMEOUT', when: 'The Evaluator didn’t answer within timeoutMs' },
  { reason: 'NETWORK_ERROR', when: 'The Evaluator is unreachable' },
  { reason: 'MALFORMED_CONTEXT', when: 'A context attribute has the wrong type or format' },
  { reason: 'MALFORMED_FLAG', when: 'The flag’s targeting refers to a variation it doesn’t have' },
  { reason: 'EVALUATOR_ERROR', when: 'The Evaluator answered with another error, or with something else' },
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="SDK and NestJS guide"
  description="Evaluate flags from TypeScript with @ctff/sdk, and from a NestJS app with @ctff/nestjs: variation, variationDetail, allFlags, @Flags() and @RequireFlag."
>
  <section class="section">
    <h1 class="ff-serif text-3xl">SDK and NestJS guide</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p class="text-lg">
        <code>@ctff/sdk</code> evaluates flags through an Evaluator, for a context you give it.
        <code>@ctff/nestjs</code> configures it once for a NestJS app, and builds the context from each request.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both are TypeScript packages of the repository’s pnpm workspace. V1 publishes nothing to a registry, so
        depend on them from the workspace (<code>"@ctff/sdk": "workspace:*"</code>), as the Birdwatch API does.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">Evaluate a flag with @ctff/sdk</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Create one client per environment with <code>createClient</code>: the Evaluator’s base URL and the
        environment’s SDK key, from the admin app. It throws a <code>TypeError</code> at once for a missing URL or
        SDK key, or a timeout that isn’t a positive number, so configuration mistakes show up at startup. There is no
        local cache: every call is one request to the Evaluator.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeSample lang="ts" title="evaluate.ts" code={evaluate} />
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <code>variation(flagKey, context, fallbackValue)</code> resolves to the served variation.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>variationDetail(flagKey, context, fallbackValue)</code> adds the variation index (<code>null</code>
          when the fallback value is returned) and the evaluation reason.
        </li>
        <li>
          <code>allFlags(context)</code> is a bulk evaluation of every flag of the environment that isn’t archived:
          <code>values</code> holds every flag that served a variation, <code>details</code> every flag with its
          evaluation, and <code>error</code> is <code>null</code> unless the Evaluator couldn’t be asked.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The context is <code>{'{ user_id?: string; country?: string }'}</code>. Either attribute may be left out,
        and a rule on a missing attribute never matches.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Table caption="Evaluation reasons when a variation is served" columns={[
      { key: 'reason', label: 'Reason' },
      { key: 'when', label: 'When' },
    ]} rows={servedReasons} />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">The fallback value</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        <code>variation</code>, <code>variationDetail</code> and <code>allFlags</code> never throw or reject. When no
        variation can be served, <code>variation</code> and <code>variationDetail</code> return the fallback value
        you passed, with one of these reasons; the errors come as
        <code>{"{ kind: 'ERROR', errorKind }"}</code>. <code>allFlags</code> has no fallback value: when the
        Evaluator can’t be asked, <code>values</code> is empty and <code>error</code> names the problem, so look
        flags up with your own, as in <code>all.values['photo-id-beta'] ?? false</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Table caption="When the fallback value is returned" columns={[
      { key: 'reason', label: 'Reason or error kind' },
      { key: 'when', label: 'When' },
    ]} rows={fallbackReasons} />
    <Alert variant="info" icon="info" title="Not the default variation">
      The default variation is part of a flag’s targeting, and is served (reason <code>DEFAULT</code>) when
      targeting is on and no individual target or rule matched. The fallback value belongs to your code, and is
      returned only when no variation could be served at all. Pick the fallback value your feature can live with
      if the Evaluator is down.
    </Alert>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">NestJS: set up @ctff/nestjs</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        First, a <code>CtffContextResolver</code>, which builds the context to evaluate flags for from a request.
        It is created inside <code>CtffModule</code>, so it can inject global providers and those of the modules
        you pass in <code>imports</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeSample lang="ts" title="birder-context.resolver.ts" code={contextResolver} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Then import <code>CtffModule</code> once, in the root module, with <code>forRoot()</code> and the same
        options as <code>createClient</code>:
      </p>
    </div>
    <CodeSample lang="ts" title="app.module.ts" code={appModule} />
    <div class="prose">
      <p>To configure it from injected providers, use <code>forRootAsync()</code>:</p>
    </div>
    <CodeSample lang="ts" title="app.module.ts, with forRootAsync()" code={appAsyncModule} />
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <h2 class="ff-serif text-2xl">NestJS: use flags in controllers</h2>
    <CodeSample lang="ts" title="sighting.controller.ts" code={sightingController} />
    <div class="prose">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong><code>CtffClient</code></strong> is the SDK’s client, injected by its class, for a context you
          build yourself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>@Flags()</code></strong> injects the result of <code>allFlags</code> for the context the
          resolver builds from the request. However many times <code>@Flags()</code> and <code>@RequireFlag</code>
          are used in a request, it makes one Evaluator call, and nothing is cached across requests. Evaluator
          problems never fail the request.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>@RequireFlag(flagKey)</code></strong>, on a route or a controller, answers
          <code>404 Not Found</code> unless the flag serves <code>true</code>. A flag that serves no variation
          (unknown, archived, or the Evaluator unreachable) counts as <code>false</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The resolver runs once per request, the first time the flags are needed, so whatever it reads, such as
        <code>request.user</code>, must be set by middleware or a guard that runs before. Decorators apply from the
        bottom up: in <code>@RequireFlag('photo-id-beta') @UseGuards(AuthGuard)</code>, the auth guard runs first.
        An exception thrown by the resolver fails the request.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
